  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "R/O C-5/9 vs Food Inspector on 28 November, 2016 1 In The Court Of Sh. Rakesh Pandit, Asj-01, New Delhi District, Patiala House Courts, New Delhi Ca No. 8513/16 Charan Das S/o Late Sh. Shri Ram M/s Aggaral Sweet India, 139-140, Yamuna Vihar Road, North Ghonda, Delhi-110053. R/o C-5/9, Yamuna Vihar, Delhi-110053. ....Appellant Vs. Food Inspector Department of Pfa Govt. Of Nct of Delhi A-20 Lawrence Road Industrial Area, Delhi-110035. .... Respondent Date of receiving of Appeal : 22.04.2016 Date of arguments : 07.11.2016 Date of judgment : 28.11.2016 Judgment",
    "facts": ". 2 The brief facts of the case as per record and from trial court record are that accused is running an establishment namely M/s Aggarwal Sweet India, at 139-140, Yamuna Vihar, North Ghonda, Delhi-110053. On 19.01.2009 at about 5.00 p.m. Food Inspector Sh. Ranjeet Singh visited the said place and purchased 750 grams of \"Vegetable Chutney\" (ready for sale) from accused. FI was accompanied with SDM/LHA Sh. A. K. Sharma. The said Vegetable Chutney was purchased for the purposes of analysis under PFA Act. The same sample was divided into three parts, packed as per Rules of PFA. Panchanama was prepared. One sample was sent for analysis to Public Analyst. As per the report of Analyst, sample did not confirm to the standard because \"The sample is adulterated with synthetic colouring matter viz. Tartrazine and Brilliant Blue FCF\" R/O C-5/9 vs Food Inspector on 28 November, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/119904759/ 1 3 As the sample was failed, prosecution was launched by Food Inspector by filing complaint dated 24.04.2009 with the court. Accused was summoned. He has exercised his right u/sec.13(2) PFA Act for getting the sample analyzed from CFL. CFL reported that \"I am of the opinion that the above sample contains synthetic food colour i.e. Tartrazine, Brilliant Blue FCF & hence contravenes Rule 29 of PFA Rules 1955 as per tests performed. Charan Das Vs. State 4 Charge was framed against accused u/sec.2(ia) (b) (j) & (m) read with sec.7 of PFA Act, 1954 punishable u/sec.16(1A) of PFA Act. 5 In this appeal, the accused has assailed the judgment on the following grounds:- (i) Non-compliance of Sec.10(7) of PFA Act and Rule i.e. independent witnesses were not joined. (ii) Non-compliance of Rule 14 of PFA Rules i.e. the bottles, spoon etc., were not made clean and dried at the spot by the food inspector or any other official (iii) There is variation in PA and CFL reports and thus benefit goes in favour of appellant. ( iv ) It is argued that the colour up to 100 PPM is allowed in vegetable chutney as per appendix C of PFA Rules, 1995 (argued orally). (v) After coming into Food Safety & Standards Act, the punishment is to be granted under the said Act (argued orally). 7 No reply was filed by respondent/state and the matter was argued orally.",
    "arguments": "9 It is argued by counsel for appellant that no public witness was present in the proceedings nor efforts were made to join any public witnesses.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "9 It is argued by counsel for appellant that no public witness was present in the proceedings nor efforts were made to join any public witnesses.",
